1
00:00:07,799 --> 00:00:13,978
There are eleven places on this planet where nobody
has jurisdiction over anything, and

2
00:00:13,998 --> 00:00:16,330
Albert Wesker bought two of them.

3
00:00:16,730 --> 00:00:23,639
The first was an island. It appears on charts as
Rockfort, a British Overseas Territory

4
00:00:23,659 --> 00:00:30,728
dependency of four thousand hectares, uninhabited
since a whaling station closed in 1961,

5
00:00:30,748 --> 00:00:37,498
leased in perpetuity to an Umbrella subsidiary in
2011 for the purpose of atmospheric

6
00:00:37,518 --> 00:00:38,214
research.

7
00:00:38,613 --> 00:00:44,583
There is a great deal of documentation about that
lease. There is a debate about it in

8
00:00:44,603 --> 00:00:50,643
the House of Lords in 2013, in Hansard, with four
peers speaking. The narrator has read

9
00:00:50,663 --> 00:00:54,403
it. It is completely convincing and
it never happened.

10
00:00:54,804 --> 00:01:00,834
What is on Rockfort now: a deep-water quay, an
airstrip that takes heavy transports, four

11
00:01:00,854 --> 00:01:06,885
hundred personnel, and thirteen levels cut into the
rock beneath the old whaling station.

12
00:01:07,284 --> 00:01:12,084
What is done on Rockfort: everything that
cannot be done in Delaware.

13
00:01:12,484 --> 00:01:18,473
The narrator wishes to be precise, because this is the
chapter where the story of a company

14
00:01:18,493 --> 00:01:24,415
becomes the story of something else, and precision is
the only thing that keeps it honest.

15
00:01:24,814 --> 00:01:32,278
Umbrella's public research was real. Aeterna was
real. The assay was real, the antiviral

16
00:01:32,298 --> 00:01:36,614
was real, Ravenna happened. None
of it was a front.

17
00:01:37,015 --> 00:01:43,370
But from 2037, in thirteen levels of rock in the
South Atlantic and fourteen levels of

18
00:01:43,390 --> 00:01:49,894
ice in Antarctica, Dr. Evelyn Marsh and Dr. William
Birkin were also doing work that had

19
00:01:49,914 --> 00:01:56,269
no product, no publication, no oversight and no
purpose that either of them would have

20
00:01:56,289 --> 00:02:00,125
been able to defend in a sentence
to anyone outside.

21
00:02:00,525 --> 00:02:02,305
They were building organisms.

22
00:02:02,704 --> 00:02:09,780
Birkin's programme was called Structural Biology and it
was, in his own description, *soldiers

23
00:02:09,800 --> 00:02:11,214
after the soldier*.

24
00:02:11,735 --> 00:02:16,243
The Tyrant is a magnificent dead end. It's a
nineteen-seventies answer — take a person,

25
00:02:16,263 --> 00:02:20,719
flood them with a virus, select the one in a million
who survives and stabilise what's

26
00:02:20,739 --> 00:02:25,195
left. Brutally wasteful, and every one of them is a
hand-made object. You can't have a

27
00:02:25,215 --> 00:02:27,225
hundred of something you can't specify.

28
00:02:27,625 --> 00:02:33,614
I specified it. In another life, with a team
of forty, for eight years.

29
00:02:34,014 --> 00:02:38,240
And you got *one*, Albert. One at a time, each of
them slightly different, each of them

30
00:02:38,260 --> 00:02:41,704
needing its own conditioning. That's not
biology, that's blacksmithing.

31
00:02:42,104 --> 00:02:48,548
What Birkin proposed instead was a scaffold: a
designed organism, grown from a defined

32
00:02:48,568 --> 00:02:55,763
line, with the viral element engineered in from the
beginning rather than survived. Predictable.

33
00:02:55,783 --> 00:03:01,025
Repeatable. Documentable, in nine hundred
pages, with a specification.

34
00:03:01,425 --> 00:03:07,381
It took him four years and it produced, in 2041, the
T-103 line that the Umbrella Security

35
00:03:07,401 --> 00:03:13,357
Service eventually deployed in numbers, and the
narrator notes that the difference between

36
00:03:13,377 --> 00:03:19,201
the thing in the Lyon stadium and the things that
came out of Rockfort is the difference

37
00:03:19,221 --> 00:03:24,978
between a cathedral and a housing estate: one is a
miracle, the other can be ordered by

38
00:03:24,998 --> 00:03:25,774
the hundred.

39
00:03:26,175 --> 00:03:30,685
Wesker walked that hall in 2041
and said one thing.

40
00:03:31,205 --> 00:03:32,034
How many?

41
00:03:32,435 --> 00:03:36,646
Six a month, at this site. Forty if we build the
Antarctic hall out. More if you want

42
00:03:36,666 --> 00:03:37,044
a plant.

43
00:03:37,444 --> 00:03:43,039
And Albert Wesker, who had spent thirty-one years
in another world trying to make one

44
00:03:43,059 --> 00:03:48,653
of these, stood in a corridor a hundred and forty
metres under an island in the South

45
00:03:48,673 --> 00:03:54,531
Atlantic and gave an instruction that would put forty
thousand of them on eleven planets.

46
00:03:55,051 --> 00:03:56,261
Build the hall.

47
00:03:56,662 --> 00:04:01,431
Evelyn Marsh's programme had no name at
all, and that was deliberate.

48
00:04:01,831 --> 00:04:07,873
She was thirty-eight. She had been given the ice at
thirty-two and had not left it for

49
00:04:07,893 --> 00:04:14,075
more than eleven days at a time since. Six hundred
and eleven staff who could not refuse

50
00:04:14,095 --> 00:04:20,348
her. Four thousand cradles. A budget that Catherine
Adeyemi had stopped itemising in 2035

51
00:04:20,368 --> 00:04:26,621
at Wesker's instruction and that Carrick raised,
formally, in writing, every single year.

52
00:04:27,021 --> 00:04:32,999
What she was doing was this: she was taking the thing
that had made Aeterna — the factor

53
00:04:33,019 --> 00:04:38,929
in a vial labelled *ODD* that uncoupled a cell's
damage signal from its clock — and she

54
00:04:38,949 --> 00:04:44,724
was trying to build the same uncoupling into a
whole human being, from conception, as

55
00:04:44,744 --> 00:04:45,951
a heritable trait.

56
00:04:46,350 --> 00:04:48,821
Not a drug. Not a therapy. A *lineage*.

57
00:04:49,341 --> 00:04:54,205
Aeterna is a crutch. You take it every eighteen weeks
for the rest of your life and when

58
00:04:54,225 --> 00:04:58,978
you stop, you fall over. That's not what we want and
it was never what we wanted, it's

59
00:04:58,998 --> 00:05:00,311
just what we could sell.

60
00:05:00,711 --> 00:05:03,821
It is what has funded you for nine years.

61
00:05:04,221 --> 00:05:09,253
I know. I'm not being ungrateful, I'm being
*accurate*. What I want is a person who doesn't

62
00:05:09,273 --> 00:05:13,751
need it. Who is born not needing it. Who passes
not needing it to their children.

63
00:05:14,480 --> 00:05:17,371
And what does the System call that?

64
00:05:17,771 --> 00:05:23,884
She could not see the System. She had known it
existed since 2029, in the way one knows

65
00:05:23,904 --> 00:05:26,281
a country exists from photographs.

66
00:05:26,800 --> 00:05:29,971
It calls it Progenitor.

67
00:05:30,370 --> 00:05:31,600
Then that's what I'm doing.

68
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:38,533
The narrator notes that the ethical structures which
would ordinarily have made this impossible

69
00:05:38,553 --> 00:05:44,534
did exist on earth in 2038, in every country, in
considerable detail, and that they had

70
00:05:44,554 --> 00:05:50,810
no purchase whatsoever on fourteen levels of ice in a
place where no state has jurisdiction

71
00:05:50,830 --> 00:05:56,190
and where every member of staff was incapable of
reporting anything to anyone.

72
00:05:56,591 --> 00:06:03,661
Carrick's memorandum of 2038 is four pages long. The
narrator will quote the last line.

73
00:06:04,180 --> 00:06:10,250
There is no law here. I am the only oversight that
exists at this site, I report to the

74
00:06:10,270 --> 00:06:16,270
man funding it, and I am therefore not oversight. I
am a witness. I am putting that in

75
00:06:16,290 --> 00:06:19,910
writing so that it is on a piece of
paper somewhere.

76
00:06:20,310 --> 00:06:26,177
Wesker read it, wrote *noted, and overruled* at the
bottom in his own hand, signed it,

77
00:06:26,197 --> 00:06:28,983
and put it in the drawer with the others.

78
00:06:29,384 --> 00:06:35,149
There is one conversation from that year that
matters more than all the science, and it

79
00:06:35,169 --> 00:06:38,674
happened outside, in the wind,
between two buildings.

80
00:06:39,194 --> 00:06:43,785
I want to ask you for something and I want you to
say no properly instead of putting it

81
00:06:43,805 --> 00:06:44,314
off again.

82
00:06:44,714 --> 00:06:45,485
Ask.

83
00:06:45,884 --> 00:06:50,884
Affinity. Whatever the number is that you have and I
don't. I've watched you for eight

84
00:06:50,904 --> 00:06:56,195
years. You don't get colds. You don't get older. In
2033 you put your hand through a window

85
00:06:56,215 --> 00:07:01,214
at Arklay because a door was stuck and it was healed
by dinner, and Annette and I have

86
00:07:01,234 --> 00:07:03,081
discussed it four hundred times.

87
00:07:03,362 --> 00:07:08,453
I'm thirty-eight. I've got maybe twenty-five good
years of work left and I need forty.

88
00:07:09,293 --> 00:07:09,963
No.

89
00:07:10,362 --> 00:07:12,193
Give me the *reason*, not the answer.

90
00:07:12,593 --> 00:07:19,360
The reason is that in 2029 I told you what happens
to the person who takes what I took

91
00:07:19,380 --> 00:07:26,306
— nine hundred and ninety-nine thousand nine hundred
and ninety-nine deaths per million,

92
00:07:26,326 --> 00:07:33,252
and the survivor stops wanting what they used to
want. And you said *can I have it*, and

93
00:07:33,272 --> 00:07:39,961
I said no, and you said *when I'm sixty*, and I
said only if you build a version that

94
00:07:39,981 --> 00:07:41,303
does not do that.

95
00:07:41,583 --> 00:07:47,977
It is 2038. You have not built it. You have built
four things that are extremely close

96
00:07:47,997 --> 00:07:54,316
and one of them killed twenty-four primates in a
way that I will not describe. So the

97
00:07:54,336 --> 00:08:00,955
answer is still no, and it will be no until the day
you put a version in front of me that

98
00:08:00,975 --> 00:08:04,013
I would be willing to give to a stranger.

99
00:08:04,413 --> 00:08:05,483
And if I never do?

100
00:08:05,882 --> 00:08:11,869
Then you will die in about fifty years having been
the second-best biologist who ever

101
00:08:11,889 --> 00:08:12,293
lived.

102
00:08:12,692 --> 00:08:18,387
She walked in silence for a while, in a coat with a
crimson iris on the shoulder, with

103
00:08:18,407 --> 00:08:21,643
fourteen hundred miles of ice in
every direction.

104
00:08:22,163 --> 00:08:25,953
(very soft) That's the first time you've
ever refused me anything.

105
00:08:26,353 --> 00:08:27,323
Yes.

106
00:08:27,723 --> 00:08:29,993
Do you know what that does to a
person like me?

107
00:08:30,392 --> 00:08:34,562
Yes. I have been counting on it
for eight years.

108
00:08:34,962 --> 00:08:41,472
The narrator asks you to remember that exchange,
because in 2043 Dr. Evelyn Marsh does

109
00:08:41,492 --> 00:08:48,153
build it, and it does work, and she is the first
person to take it, and she does not ask

110
00:08:48,173 --> 00:08:48,913
him first.

111
00:08:49,312 --> 00:08:54,880
The narrator has an obligation here and is going to
discharge it in one page, because

112
00:08:54,900 --> 00:09:00,468
it would be dishonest to spend fifty chapters on a
man's manners and skip what was in

113
00:09:00,488 --> 00:09:01,323
the building.

114
00:09:01,722 --> 00:09:08,290
The B.O.W. programme at Rockfort between 2037 and
2044 produced eleven organism lines.

115
00:09:08,310 --> 00:09:14,954
Four were soldiers. Three were tools — searchers,
carriers, things for hard vacuum. Two

116
00:09:14,974 --> 00:09:21,695
were failures that had to be ended, and the record of
how is sealed and the narrator has

117
00:09:21,715 --> 00:09:23,993
not been permitted to read it.

118
00:09:24,393 --> 00:09:30,543
The remaining two are the reason the Hangzhou Accord
has a clause about biology at all.

119
00:09:30,942 --> 00:09:37,148
Every one of them was grown from a defined line.
None was made from a person. Wesker's

120
00:09:37,168 --> 00:09:43,590
instruction on that, issued in 2037 and never
amended, is four words long — *nothing that

121
00:09:43,610 --> 00:09:50,033
was human* — and Birkin argued against it every year
for a decade on the entirely correct

122
00:09:50,053 --> 00:09:55,173
grounds that the human substrate was more
capable, and lost every year.

123
00:09:55,692 --> 00:10:00,903
The T-103 line tops out because we are building
from scratch. If I take a volunteer —

124
00:10:01,302 --> 00:10:02,712
(very cold) No.

125
00:10:03,113 --> 00:10:07,603
A *volunteer*, Albert, an informed adult, we have
four hundred applications from people

126
00:10:07,623 --> 00:10:07,863
who —

127
00:10:08,263 --> 00:10:15,654
(very cold) I have read the applications. Nine of them
are from people with terminal diagnoses,

128
00:10:15,674 --> 00:10:22,286
and one is from a man whose daughter needs money,
and every one of them is the reason

129
00:10:22,306 --> 00:10:23,613
the answer is no.

130
00:10:24,332 --> 00:10:31,567
Will. In the other place, I did this. I did it for
eight years with a research budget

131
00:10:31,587 --> 00:10:38,906
and an ethics board I had dissolved, and every
single one of them was a volunteer, and

132
00:10:38,926 --> 00:10:44,283
I can still tell you their names, and I
have no idea why I can.

133
00:10:44,893 --> 00:10:49,682
(very soft) Nothing that was human. Put it
on the wall of the wing.

134
00:10:50,082 --> 00:10:56,017
It is on the wall of the wing. It is still there, in
a facility on an island in the South

135
00:10:56,037 --> 00:11:01,704
Atlantic that is now a Compact xenobiology station,
and every species that has worked

136
00:11:01,724 --> 00:11:06,923
in that building for two hundred years has asked
what it means, and been told.

137
00:11:08,072 --> 00:11:16,104
Status. Level ninety. Viral Affinity thirty-four.
The requirement for Origin is forty.

138
00:11:16,124 --> 00:11:24,156
Ninety-four facilities. Four hundred and eleven
thousand personnel, sixty-one thousand

139
00:11:24,176 --> 00:11:32,583
Absolute. Market capitalisation: four point one
trillion dollars. Next tier: Conglomerate.

140
00:11:32,603 --> 00:11:34,361
Absorb an industry.

141
00:11:34,951 --> 00:11:36,641
Six points of Affinity.

142
00:11:37,041 --> 00:11:37,871
Six.

143
00:11:38,270 --> 00:11:43,321
Three levels' worth of stat points, if
I spend nothing else.

144
00:11:43,721 --> 00:11:50,918
Yes. Or one purchase, in the Viral tab, at a price
you can now afford six times over.

145
00:11:51,703 --> 00:11:55,774
You have never once offered me a
shortcut before.

146
00:11:56,173 --> 00:12:02,856
(flat) It is not a shortcut. It is the correct price,
and you are the first host to reach

147
00:12:02,876 --> 00:12:06,245
this line with the temperament
to be told it.

148
00:12:06,647 --> 00:12:12,664
The narrator notes that Albert Wesker did not buy it
that night, or that year. He spent

149
00:12:12,684 --> 00:12:18,841
his stat points in threes for another twenty-four
years, the slow way, like a man walking

150
00:12:18,861 --> 00:12:21,616
up a mountain he has been told is there.

151
00:12:22,017 --> 00:12:28,466
The vault at Arklay was opened on the fourth of
December 2062, twenty-four years after

152
00:12:28,486 --> 00:12:35,087
this conversation, and everything in the next nine
chapters is the reason for the delay.

153
00:12:35,486 --> 00:12:38,876
From somewhere behind his eyes, the
passenger spoke.

154
00:12:39,397 --> 00:12:41,421
You are frightened of it.

155
00:12:41,821 --> 00:12:48,216
I am *careful* with it. I have been offered a thing
that permanently changes what I am,

156
00:12:48,236 --> 00:12:54,558
by an entity that has never once explained its own
interest, and I intend to be at the

157
00:12:54,578 --> 00:13:00,531
top of every other ladder in the world before I
take the step I cannot take back.

158
00:13:00,931 --> 00:13:02,785
And if it is a trap?

159
00:13:03,184 --> 00:13:08,834
Then it will be the first one either of us ever
walked into with our eyes open.
